          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have stage 3 kidney disease and high blood pressure. Does the classification link the two, and what target does guidance describe for someone like me?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure and kidney disease are recognized as linked conditions (consultation). The classification system used for diseases (ICD-10) presumes a causal connection between high blood pressure (hypertension) and chronic kidney disease, meaning they are often paired together without needing extra information (coding-hypertension-i10). Kidney disease can also be a cause of high blood pressure (condition-hypertension, consultation). Managing blood pressure is an important part of care when kidney disease is present (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets, research).\n\nFor managing blood pressure in people with chronic kidney disease, guidance describes a blood pressure target typically below 130/80 mm Hg for most individuals (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, consultation; guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, research). Some guidance also suggests renin-angiotensin blockade as a preferred treatment approach to help manage blood pressure for individuals with chronic kidney disease (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, research).\n\nYou should discuss these classifications and specific treatment targets and options with your clinician for personalized guidance on your condition and management plan (consultation, research).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
